# Forbidden patterns for Python files (*.py). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: py
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB — patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation
# (e.g. `(TODO|FIXME|XXX)`). Patterns cannot contain a literal TAB.
# The `scaffold-extensions:` header above tells check-patterns which file
# extensions these rules scan — drop a new <lang>.txt with its own header to
# add a language; see forbidden-patterns/README.md.
# Installed as `.forbidden-patterns/backend.txt`.

(^|[^A-Za-z_])print[[:space:]]*\(	Use structlog (or the project's logger), not print()
os\.path\.join	Use pathlib.Path(), not os.path.join
(^|[^A-Za-z_])breakpoint[[:space:]]*\(	Remove breakpoint() before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])pdb\.set_trace[[:space:]]*\(	Remove pdb.set_trace() before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])ipdb\.set_trace[[:space:]]*\(	Remove ipdb.set_trace() before committing
\.utcnow[[:space:]]*\(	datetime.utcnow() is deprecated (3.12+) — use datetime.now(datetime.UTC)
\.utcfromtimestamp[[:space:]]*\(	datetime.utcfromtimestamp() is deprecated (3.12+) — use datetime.fromtimestamp(ts, tz=datetime.UTC)
(^|[^A-Za-z_])verify[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*False($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	requests/httpx verify=False disables TLS cert validation (MITM risk) — fix the cert/CA, don't disable verification

# --- Linter / type-checker escape hatches (the Python twin of frontend.txt's
# @ts-ignore / @ts-nocheck rules) ---
# A CODED suppression stays legal and is the intended escape valve:
# `# type: ignore[arg-type]` and `# noqa: E501` name what they silence and are
# reviewable. Only the blanket forms are banned. A file-level `# ruff: noqa`
# silences every group ruff.toml selects — including the curated S-codes
# (shell=True, eval, hardcoded password) — for the whole file at a green exit;
# `# mypy: ignore-errors` is @ts-nocheck by another name; a bare `# type: ignore`
# hides every error on the line forever, including ones a later refactor
# introduces. Each pattern starts with `(^|[[:space:]])` because check-patterns
# drops any rule line whose FIRST character is `#` as a file comment.
(^|[[:space:]])#[[:space:]]*type:[[:space:]]*ignore($|[^[])	Bare `# type: ignore` hides every error on the line — use the coded form, e.g. `# type: ignore[arg-type]`
(^|[[:space:]])#[[:space:]]*mypy:[[:space:]]*ignore-errors	File-level `# mypy: ignore-errors` turns type-checking off for the whole module — fix the types or ignore the specific error code
(^|[[:space:]])#[[:space:]]*ruff:[[:space:]]*noqa($|[^:])	File-level `# ruff: noqa` disables every selected rule for the file — suppress one code on one line instead, e.g. `# noqa: S602`

# --- Coverage escape hatch ---
# .coveragerc leaves coverage.py's default exclude_lines active, so this pragma
# deletes the line from the XML report entirely and diff-cover, which has no data
# for it, scores --coverage-gate at 100% on code that never ran. Eleven characters
# on a `def` line, cheaper than the assertion-free test, and invisible in the CI
# log (unlike a lowered threshold). Genuinely unexecutable code — a platform
# branch this runner can never take — takes `scaffold-allow` and is named in the
# handoff summary like any other suppression.
(^|[[:space:]])#[[:space:]]*pragma:[[:space:]]*no[[:space:]]*cover	Coverage pragma removes the line from the patch-coverage gate — write the test, or scaffold-allow a genuinely unexecutable branch

# Optional: unfinished stub — the Python twin of rust.txt's todo!()/unimplemented!()
# and kotlin.txt's TODO() rules. `raise NotImplementedError` inhabits any return
# type, so a stub type-checks, lints, and is fully covered by a test that asserts
# only the raise: every mechanical signal reads as complete while nothing is
# implemented. OFF by default because it is also the canonical body of an
# @abstractmethod, and the decorator sits on a PREVIOUS line where a line-oriented
# grep cannot see it — an always-on rule would fire on correct ABCs. Enable it in
# app code with no abstract base classes; mark real abstract methods
# `# scaffold-allow: abstract`.
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])raise[[:space:]]+NotImplementedError	Unfinished stub left in — implement it, or scaffold-allow an abstract-method placeholder

# Optional: asyncio.get_event_loop() raises RuntimeError on 3.14+ when called with
# no running loop. OFF by default: inside a coroutine/callback it correctly returns
# the running loop and is legitimate, and library code targeting 3.9–3.13 uses it
# deliberately — a name-anchored ban over-fires on correct code. Enable for app
# code that should standardize on asyncio.run()/get_running_loop().
# asyncio\.get_event_loop[[:space:]]*\(	Use asyncio.run() or asyncio.get_running_loop(), not get_event_loop()

# Optional: 12-factor config (III). Mirror of the active frontend.txt rule, but
# OFF by default here because Python test clients legitimately target localhost
# (httpx.AsyncClient(base_url="http://localhost"), Starlette TestClient). Enable
# it for multi-service backends; mark deliberate test lines with `scaffold-allow`.
# https?://(localhost|127\.0\.0\.1)	No hardcoded localhost/127.0.0.1 URLs — read from config/env

# Optional: forbid TODO/FIXME/XXX in one line via regex alternation.
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(TODO|FIXME|XXX)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	TODO/FIXME/XXX not allowed — open a ticket instead
#
# Optional (PCRE only — ERE doesn't support lookahead): flag TODO/FIXME/XXX
# only when missing a ticket ref like (PROJ-123). Requires grep -P (GNU only).
# (TODO|FIXME|XXX)(?!\s*\([A-Z]+-[0-9]+\))	TODO without ticket ref
